  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through Monday)\nIssued at 230 AM CDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nEnsemble and deterministic guidance indicate a pattern change is \nlikely by the upcoming weekend as a shortwave trough amplifies and \ndigs southeastward on the northeast periphery of the ridge. The \nridge will retrograde to the west, which will bring in stronger \nnortherly flow aloft into the Southern Plains. \n\nAn initial cold front/northerly wind shift will move in Friday \nmorning. However, the cold air advection with the initial wind \nshift will likely be limited/negligible. There will be a chance of\nshowers and thunderstorms with the front with the highest chance \nacross north central Oklahoma. Highs Friday afternoon will likely \nstill reach the 100s deg F across most locations (unless\nshower/storm coverage is more widespread.\n\nA stronger, reinforcing cold front will advance southward in tandem \nwith the shortwave trough late Friday into Saturday, bringing \ncooler/less hot weather into the area. Even with a stronger front, \nthe most appreciable cold air advection will remain to our \nnortheast. As a result, the most significant cool down will be \nacross north central Oklahoma. Across southwest Oklahoma into \nwestern north Texas, the cool down will be more limited. With \nsynoptic-scale forcing remaining to the northeast, rain chances are \nvery low/near zero.\n\nHigh temperatures Saturday afternoon are forecast to range from the \nlow 90s (north central Oklahoma) to around 100 deg F (southwest \nOklahoma/western north Texas). This relative cool down will continue \ninto Sunday before warmer weather returns on Monday.\n\nMahale",
